# The Mischief-Maker!
Tamil title: குறும்புக்காரன்!

## Image 1: 001-d571fc2fd1.png

### A Scene the Camera Will Not Accept

**Place:** The rest house at the edge of the village

**Present:** A young man, who is a reformer, and the village elders.

[The village elders are angry with the young man because he criticises ancient horoscopes and palm-leaf texts. They cannot answer the questions he asks. Nor can they refute the reasons he gives! “He certainly speaks very cleverly!”

“A city education!”

“A loudmouth and a bully!”

“The scoundrel will split an atom and show you every last particle!”

With remarks of this kind, they express their fond irritation with him.

The young man sees one of the village elders...]

**Young Man:** Elder brother! There is something I wanted to ask you...

**Elder:** When have you ever gone without asking something, boy? Very well, what subject have you found now?

**Young Man:** If you are angry, never mind. I shall speak only if you are calm...

**Elder:** Why, boy? Am I such a hot-tempered man?

**Young Man:** I did not say that. There is a proverb about taking one's anger at something out on the ox...

## Image 2: 002-9140f9f5bb.png

That is why I was afraid that if you were angry for some other reason, you might take it out on me...

**Elder:** I am not angry with anyone. Tell me, tell me...

**Young Man:** When a son speaks disgracefully about the mother who bore him, good and respectable people would surely condemn him, would they not? Their hearts would tremble: “This worthless fellow abuses his mother! His arrogance must be brought under control. It is a sin even to look upon his face!” That is how good people would speak, is it not?

**Elder:** Yes... Everyone would revile him...

**Young Man:** “You speak contemptuously of the mother who gave birth to you and raised you, who protected you so that not even a fly or an ant settled upon you, and who cherished, praised and nurtured you, you depraved fool!” They would heap up reasons and scold him, would they not?

**Elder:** Yes. What doubt is there?

**Young Man:** Suppose that same son does not stop with abusing his mother, but strikes her as well.

**Elder:** Bah! Is he even human?

**Young Man:** The world would not respect him. Is that not so?

**Elder:** Yes! It would spit upon him...

**Young Man:** Suppose that son does not stop with abuse and assault, but actually cuts off his mother's head...

**Elder:** [Startled] Accursed wretch! What, what? He cut off his mother's head? Who is that monster? Where is he?

**Young Man:** Wait, elder! I merely asked what the world would think and say about a man if he did such a thing, and you roar before me, “Bring him here!” You fool... If a son cuts off his own mother's head, what would the world say about that son? Tell me.

## Image 3: 003-7765d2d9de.png

**Elder:** The world would call him a great sinner, a vile man, a murderer...

**Young Man:** It would hurl at him every word of abuse it could find.

**Elder:** We would have to summon Adisesha, with his thousand tongues, to curse him...

**Young Man:** Such would be its fury.

**Elder:** How could it be otherwise? Would the world spare the great sinner who cut off the head of the mother who bore him?

**Young Man:** A snake must be killed when it is seen; a scorpion must be crushed. Likewise, the son who kills his mother...?

**Elder:** A son, is he? He is a great sinner!

**Young Man:** Quite so. If you were to see that great sinner who was her son...?

**Elder:** He must be hacked into pieces and served as a feast to the crows, dogs and jackals...

**Young Man:** You have pronounced judgment, elder! Are you not an elder? Would you speak unjustly?

**Elder:** Is it only I? The whole world would pronounce the same judgment.

**Young Man:** Very well. Now watch: both your judgment and the world's judgment will change in a single instant...

**Elder:** What is this absurd talk? If a son cuts off his own mother's head...

**Young Man:** You have already explained it at length! Imagine the crows, vultures, dogs and jackals all waiting; knives and sickles have been put into your hands; and the man who cut off his mother's head has been brought and made to stand before you. If you were then told, “Go on, carry out the punishment”...

**Elder:** I would carry it out without the least hesitation...

**Young Man:** That is precisely what I say you would not do.

## Image 4: 004-d6649e091b.png

**Elder:** What? I would not carry out the punishment?

**Young Man:** Yes. You could not...

**Elder:** An absolute lie. I would certainly punish him...

**Young Man:** You simply could not...

**Elder:** Hey! What do you take me for, that you mock me like this?

[Hearing the shouting, one or two others arrive.]

**Elder:** Elder brothers! If a man killed the mother who bore him, would we let that demon of a son live without killing him? Would any of us?

**Another Elder:** Indeed not. We would stab him to death before attending to any other business...

**Elder:** This fellow says that none of us would do such a thing.

**Young Man:** I still say that you could not do it. Neither you nor that man over there could do it...

**Elder:** Are you alone capable of it, then?

**Young Man:** Did I say so? I could not do it either! Not only would we be unable to do it; we would reverse the judgment itself, fall at the feet of the man who cut off his mother's head, bow to him and plead, “Swami! Bless us. Grant that all our children and families may live in good health.”

**Elder:** To that great sinner...?

**Young Man:** Yes, to the very man who cut off his mother's head...

[At once they chase him to beat him. He runs, and they run after him shouting.]

**Elder:** You make fun of us, claiming we could not do it! Show us that sinner, boy!

**Young Man:** Come along, brave tigers! I shall show you.

[They continue the chase.]

**Elder:** Show him to us, you little jackal!

## Image 5: 005-e3f639a41f.png

[Several others join them on the way, and they all run. The young man enters narrow lanes and stops at the entrance to a Mari temple. Seeing this, the others shout:]

**The Others:** Hey! Even if you run to the farthest end of the world, we shall not let you go.

[He enters the temple. They enter too. He hides in a corner.]

**The Others:** Hey! Do you think we shall spare you because you have entered a temple? [They shout.]

[Without emerging from his hiding place, he speaks.]

**Young Man:** We are in a temple. Do you not realise it? Even now I say this truthfully, with the deity as my witness, and in the knowledge of this Mariamman: even if a man had cut off his mother's head, you would not punish him. You would only fall at his feet and worship him...

**Elder:** Get out, you fool! Come outside. You said you would show him to us. Show us, boy...

**Young Man:** Shall I show you? There, to the left of the Goddess...

[They look in that direction.]

There, on the left... a statue...

[The statue becomes visible.]

With an axe in its hand... [The axe becomes visible.]

**Young Man:** Have you seen it?

**The Others:** [Softly] Parasurama...

**Young Man:** Come, let us see you display all your courage, valour and prowess. The man who cut off his mother's head is Parasurama.

[They are dumbfounded and can say nothing.]

Well, gentlemen! Where are the roaring, shouting and threats? Where are all the brave tigers who cried that they would hack him into pieces and feed him to the crows and vultures?

## Image 6: 006-3746a4c539.png

[One of them falls down and worships.]

**One Man:** Swami! Not knowing that this fellow was speaking about you, I recklessly declared that I would do all manner of things to you. Forgive me. I am a man with a wife and children. Do not curse me...

**Young Man:** You see? The judgment has changed...

[He comes outside.]

This is what I said at the very beginning: everything appears according to the way in which we choose to look at it. When a human being does it, we call it a mortal sin. When a rishi does it, we worship the deed...

**Elder:** Enough! Enough! Go away! Talking about this and that...

**Young Man:** Did I speak of anything that did not happen?

**Elder:** Is there no difference between a deity and a human being?

**Young Man:** Who said there was no difference? There certainly is. When you thought he was a human being, you shouted that you would hack him to pieces. The moment you learnt that he was a deity, you fell down and worshipped him. How could there be no difference? But the difference is not merely between a deity and a human being. There is a difference between one human being and another as well. When you see a rickshaw puller staggering along the street, you feel like rebuking him: “Get lost, you drunken rascal!” You find the courage. But if the young heir of some rich man's household is blind drunk and measuring the road with his staggering steps, do you find the courage to rebuke him? “He is the son of an important family. He is unsteady. He may fall somewhere and hurt himself. Perhaps we should accompany him all the way home.” You even feel inclined to say that, do you not?

**Elder:** Oh, go away, brother! You have discovered some tremendous wonder. That is the way of the world.

**Young Man:** Do not call it the way of the world, elder! The world has advanced a very great deal. Call it your way. You worship a deity, do you not? Look at the qualities you see in that deity: the man who cut off his mother's head is a rishi! He has...

## Image 7: 007-d0f972c697.png

a place in the temple! Worship too! What an injustice this is! You say that deities, rishis and devotees are needed to give humanity wisdom and guide it along the right path. But when we ask about a rishi's story, this is what you show us: “Here is the righteous soul who cut off his mother's head.” If anyone asks whether that is right, your anger erupts and you roar that you will kill him...

**Elder:** Come, let us go. This fellow is always like this. He draws words out of our mouths and starts an argument. Come, come... Still, the boy has subtle intelligence and ingenuity...

**Another Elder:** If one stops and thinks it through calmly, what the boy says does seem just... Mischief-maker!

[Everyone leaves. The young man departs happily, reflecting that he has succeeded, to some extent, in making the village elders think.]

*Dravida Nadu, 26 December 1954.*
